<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saqaliba</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Saqaliba, '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a>', was the Arabic term for slaves and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freedmen">freedmen</a> of European origin in the medieval Islamic world, many taken from Slavic lands. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> they served as palace officials and soldiers of the Umayyad <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>, and some rose to high command. After the caliphate collapsed, Saqaliba leaders founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifa</a> kingdoms of their own, notably at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almeria">Almería</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denia">Denia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saqaliba.jpg" alt="Saqaliba" width="320" height="227" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sergey Ivanov · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:S._V._Ivanov._Trade_negotiations_in_the_country_of_Eastern_Slavs._Pictures_of_Russian_history._(1909).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Arabic term derives from the Greek Sklavoi and Sklavenoi, the name of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a>, and entered Arabic usage in the 7th century. It was used for the Slavic peoples of central and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and more broadly for other Eastern Europeans traded by Arab merchants and, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and Portugal, for all European slaves, including captives from raids on the Christian kingdoms. Abu Zayd al-Balkhi described three centres of the Saqaliba, Kuyaba, Slawiya and Artania, and Ibn Khordadbeh called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgars</a> kings of the Saqaliba. The Umayyad caliph Mu'awiya I settled 5,000 Slavic mercenaries who had deserted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> in the 660s.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Captives reached the Muslim world by several routes. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> sold prisoners along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga-trade-route">Volga trade route</a>, through the Khazar Kaghanate and later Volga Bulgaria to the Samanid slave market in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, from at least 786 to 1009. Venetian merchants bought war captives on the Adriatic coast of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> and carried them to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> and on to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. From <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> in the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, a major slave market between the 9th and 11th centuries, pagan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> were taken through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liutprand-of-cremona">Liutprand of Cremona</a> named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verdun">Verdun</a> as a centre of castration. Both Christians and Muslims held the enslavement of pagans lawful.</p><p>The trade to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> lost its religious legitimacy as the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> from the late 10th century, and it ended as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliphate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> disintegrated, a process completed by 1031.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Saqaliba served as servants, concubines, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuchs</a>, craftsmen, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>, slave soldiers and guards, and in al-Andalus Slavic eunuchs became so common that the word came to mean them. Most were enslaved as prepubescent children, and a European slave could sell there for as much as 1,000 dinars. In the Fatimid caliphate Ustadh Jawdhar became the senior administrator and closest confidant of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a>-caliph, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jawhar-al-siqilli">Jawhar al-Siqilli</a> also rose high. After the caliphate fell, Saqaliba leaders ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifas</a>: Mujāhid al-ʿĀmirī seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denia">Dénia</a> and extended its reach to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mallorca">Majorca</a>, and Khayran, a former slave of the caliph's palace, ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almeria">Almería</a> from 1014.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Saqaliba” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…caliphate’s armies combined Andalusi troops raised from the provinces and frontier districts with professional soldiers loyal to the ruler: the <b>Saqaliba</b>, slaves mainly of European origin who could rise to high command, and growing numbers of Berber horsemen recruited from North Africa, especially under al-Hakam II and Almanzor.</blockquote><a href="/empire/caliphate-of-cordoba">Caliphate of Córdoba · Military</a></li></ul></section>
